                                                                                                                <comments>
                            <comment id="15943845" author="iveselovskiy" created="Mon, 27 Mar 2017 19:04:03 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/apache/ignite/pull/1678&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://github.com/apache/ignite/pull/1678&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15945564" author="iveselovskiy" created="Tue, 28 Mar 2017 17:13:03 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;the test is rewritten to provide statistically justified assertions. &lt;br/&gt;
The values used in test are taken to achieve high reliability: the test will pass 1000 times with 95% probability. &lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15953114" author="vozerov" created="Mon, 3 Apr 2017 08:15:39 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=iveselovskiy&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;iveselovskiy&quot;&gt;iveselovskiy&lt;/a&gt;,&lt;br/&gt;
What kind of problem was addressed with the fix from application (not test) standpoint? Earlier &lt;tt&gt;randomLevel&lt;/tt&gt; could have return values &lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;0..32&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;, now it could return &lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;0..64&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;. How does it affect application?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15989130" author="iveselovskiy" created="Fri, 28 Apr 2017 16:42:05 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=vozerov&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;vozerov&quot;&gt;vozerov&lt;/a&gt; , the fix is that  org.apache.ignite.internal.processors.hadoop.shuffle.collections.HadoopSkipList#randomLevel returns value in range 0..Infinity, not 0..32 or 0..64. This makes the function correct. The point is that the returned value should not be limited . With very low probability it may return any positive number. And comprehensive statistic analysis performed in the test really reveals that: if you use old #randomLevel function (now it is called &quot;randomLevel32&quot;), the test will start to fail with noticeable probability.&lt;/p&gt;
</comment>
                            <comment id="16026213" author="vozerov" created="Fri, 26 May 2017 12:30:05 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Original distribution looks fine, where &quot;fine&quot; means that follows geometrical pattern on lower levels well. The problem was more with the test itself, so I removed it, as no further changes to level distribution is planned. No need for complex math in tests for this piece of code.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
